  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14255079/assets/d38a7896f4e62b72bdc73efb98732bd64bd397c12b3b343f15bf2995c1886671.png)\n\nThe graph above shows one branch of the curve $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x+C)$ for some number $C,$ where $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ and a vertical asymptote of the curve. The equation of the vertical asymptote is $x=$",
  "worked_solution": "Let's add the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ to the plot.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14255079/assets/af0cbe0471bf181c1411f2e198f19bd39cceb1b2b9c80d7222365c1445d730c8.png)\n\nTo plot the given curve, we follow these steps:\n\n- Start with the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$\n\n- Then, shift it by $1$ unit **left.**\n\nTherefore, the given graph is $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x+1),$ and so $C=1.$\n\nSince the graph is shifted by $1$ units left, the asymptote is also shifted by $1$ unit left. The corresponding asymptote on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ is $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Therefore, the equation of the asymptote shown is:\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}-1\n$$",
